{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1557374-dav20-does-anyone-have-a-good-writeup-for-the-specifics-of-what-vicissitude-can-do","id":1557374,"title":"DAV20 - does anyone have a good writeup for the specifics of what Vicissitude can do?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-01-08T05:27:39-08:00","id":1557374,"title":"DAV20 - does anyone have a good writeup for the specifics of what Vicissitude can do?","content":"In a regular table game, the limits of Vicissitude 1-3 are best handled by going &quot;ST, can I (...)&quot;. But I was wondering if anyone has a good writeup which lays out the limitations of what Vicissitude can and can't do, expanding with clarifications and rules and limitations? Ideally one that is suitable for a very granular table, or a larger setting with multiple STs etc.<br /> <br /> Ideally it would be good to have a few examples for comparison, anything that comes with testimonials or lots of playtesting etc. would be especially welcome!<br /> <br /> e.g. &quot;here is a list of Vicissitude modifications, here's how applying them to NPCs works, here's how applying them to PCs works and how much xp a PC has to pay for the modification&quot; and so on.<br /> <br /> DAV20 gives a very good explanation of what Vicissitude cannot do (it can't remove a Clan Curse, it can't heal wounds etc.), but it would be good to go a bit further since some aspects of powers 1-3, and arguably 5, leave the limits very firmly to the ST to decide, with a few examples which feel non-exhaustive. e.g. here's a thing you can do which is quite common, other uses exist.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-08T12:39:57-08:00","id":1557378,"title":"","content":"I don't have a detailed write up.<br /> <br /> There are a few things that are handy to manage ideas:<br /> 1) theoretically, you have to take from one tissue to give somewhere else. Do you want four arms? They're gonna be half the mass of your original two. Of course you need Vic 3 because you need to alter the bones, too;<br /> 2) for balance purposes, aside from increasing Appearance, any modification that increases one trait should reduce another. I don't remember what the system is exactly in Vic 2 and 3, but there's something about changing Strength and Stamina or the soak pool. Use that as the balance guide;<br /> 3) adding new mass functionally requires Vic 6;<br /> 4) a big issue is how nerves and nerve tissues are handled: to my memory, it is unclear whether Vic 1 and 2 can effectively alter nerves. On the one side, arguably any modification of flesh would also move nerves around and it would be very awkward if that change wasn't functional. On the other side, altering nerve tissue means enabling playing around with eyes, other sense organs, and to make functional additional limbs, albeit with less mass than the original, otherwise the extra limbs would hang dead from the body. Bonus question: can one turn other tissue into nerves or nerves into something else like muscle? For example, can I sacrifice the mass of a muscle to make a giant extra eye somewhere on my body, or some kind of extra brain?<br /> <br /> If you can answer these questions for your table, you can start listing possible and balanced (to some extent) modifications by allowing basically to trade a bonus on a trait or type of roll with a malus somewhere else.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-08T13:17:47-08:00","id":1557379,"title":"","content":"1) so I don't believe that Vicissitude can transfer mass from one person to another, like 4 arms on a ghoul, prior to Vicissitude 6? But that's not clear since Graft Life to Life isn't actually in DAV20, so who can say? But yeah point 1 and 3 might contradict eachother.<br /> <br /> 2) agreed that any permanent benefit applied to a PC should cost xp or dots in something else!<br /> <br /> 3) agreed, I think... but like I say that power isn't in DAV20!<br /> <br /> 4) I think nerves, immune system and so on are covered by &quot;it's magic&quot;. For instance, Vicissitude can involve putting fingers under a person's skin. Why doesn't Vicissitude cause horrific, life-ending infections? I don't think that Vicissitude can outright transform one organ or tissue type into another, since the examples are things like changing hair colour, changing eye colour, refashioning a tissue to be a different shape etc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}